<div class="p2-page">

    <!-- PHASE 2 BANNER -->
    <div class="p2-banner">
        <div class="p2-phase-pill">Phase 2 · Miasma Electrical Engine</div>
        <h1>Steam-Piston <em>Electrical</em> Drive System</h1>
        <p>
            A retrofit architecture replacing engine, fuel tank, and battery with a
            closed-loop steam piston block, high-pressure water reservoir, dual-battery
            bus, and a self-sustaining alternator recharge cycle.
        </p>
    </div>

    <!-- PHASE 2 SECTION -->
    <div class="p2-section">

        <div class="p2-section-label">Phase 2 Architecture</div>
        <h2 class="p2-section-title">Four core swaps · Dual-battery bus</h2>
        <p class="p2-section-lead">
            The Phase 2 design replaces four existing vehicle subsystems and introduces
            a separated electrical bus: Battery A drives heating; Battery B sustains
            alternator output and auxiliary loads. Steam pressure from the reservoir
            pushes pistons to rotate the crankshaft — no combustion, no spark.
        </p>

        <div class="p2-metrics">
            <div class="p2-metric"><div class="val">4</div><div class="lbl">Component swaps</div></div>
            <div class="p2-metric"><div class="val">2</div><div class="lbl">Battery buses</div></div>
            <div class="p2-metric"><div class="val">H₂O</div><div class="lbl">Working fluid</div></div>
            <div class="p2-metric"><div class="val">0</div><div class="lbl">Combustion events</div></div>
            <div class="p2-metric"><div class="val">AC/DC</div><div class="lbl">Dual-bus electrical</div></div>
        </div>

        <!-- CAR DIAGRAM -->
        <div class="p2-section-label">Vehicle Layout</div>
        <h2 class="p2-section-title">Phase 2 — Retrofitted car diagram</h2>
        <p class="p2-section-lead">
            Each numbered zone maps directly to a replaced or added component.
            The steam piston block sits where the combustion engine was; the water
            pressure reservoir replaces the fuel tank; dual batteries occupy the
            trunk/underfloor; the alternator feeds Battery B continuously.
        </p>

