    <div class="p2-section">
        <div class="p2-section-label">Engineering Roadmap</div>
        <h2 class="p2-section-title">Phase 2 — Testing milestones to make this a reality</h2>
        <p class="p2-section-lead">
            Each phase must be validated before the next. Skip no phase — steam pressure
            engineering has legally binding safety codes in most jurisdictions.
        </p>

        <div class="p2-phase-grid">
            <div class="p2-phase-card" data-num="T1">
                <h3>T-1 · Bench Boiler &amp; Pressure Test</h3>
                <p>Validate the water reservoir and heater element at rated pressure before any mechanical coupling.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Hydrostatic test vessel to 1.5× working pressure</li>
                    <li>Map heat-up time vs. PWM duty cycle</li>
                    <li>Calibrate pressure relief valve set-point</li>
                    <li>Measure thermal losses through insulation</li>
                    <li>Log pressure decay over 1-hour hold</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="p2-phase-card" data-num="T2">
                <h3>T-2 · Single-Cylinder Steam Piston Dyno</h3>
                <p>Confirm that steam pressure converts to usable crankshaft torque before scaling to multi-cylinder.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Run single cylinder on eddy-current dyno</li>
                    <li>Record torque curve 100–600 psi inlet</li>
                    <li>Measure cylinder wall temperature under load</li>
                    <li>Check piston ring seal at operating temp</li>
                    <li>Calculate indicated vs. brake thermal efficiency</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="p2-phase-card" data-num="T3">
                <h3>T-3 · Electrical Bus Validation</h3>
                <p>Verify the dual-battery AC/DC architecture holds stable voltage under load and during regen transitions.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Scope alternator AC waveform under load</li>
                    <li>Measure rectifier ripple — target &lt;100 mV</li>
                    <li>Verify buck converter holds ±1% of set voltage</li>
                    <li>Stress-test PWM at 70%, 85%, 98% duty cycles</li>
                    <li>Confirm Bat-A SOC holds under sustained heater load</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="p2-phase-card" data-num="T4">
                <h3>T-4 · Alternator Self-Sustain Loop</h3>
                <p>The critical test: does the alternator output exceed the energy consumed by the heater over a full cycle?</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Instrument every power node (Watts in, Watts out)</li>
                    <li>Run 30-minute closed-loop cycle, log SOC of both batteries</li>
                    <li>Calculate net energy surplus or deficit</li>
                    <li>Adjust transformer turn-ratio and PWM frequency</li>
                    <li>Identify parasitic loads (pump, fans, BMS, ECU)</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="p2-phase-card" data-num="T5">
                <h3>T-5 · Torque &amp; Weight Simulation</h3>
                <p>Simulate full vehicle mass, rolling resistance, and grade load with the actual piston and motor stack.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Load simulator: 3,500 lb passenger car mass baseline</li>
                    <li>Run 0–30 mph ramp and measure time vs. steam pressure</li>
                    <li>Grade simulation: 6%, 10%, 15% grades on dyno</li>
                    <li>Compare ft-lb output to diesel reference at equal weight</li>
                    <li>Measure system weight added vs. ICE removed</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="p2-phase-card" data-num="T6">
                <h3>T-6 · Closed-Loop Endurance Run</h3>
                <p>Validate water recovery, condenser efficiency, and thermal stability over an extended run.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Run 4-hour continuous loop at 55% load</li>
                    <li>Measure condenser recovery rate (% water returned)</li>
                    <li>Track reservoir level drop per hour (make-up water budget)</li>
                    <li>Monitor both battery SOC across run</li>
                    <li>Identify hotspots and vibration nodes</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ENGINEERING FLAWS -->
    <div class="p2-dark-row">
        <div class="p2-section">
            <div class="p2-section-label">Known Engineering Flaws · Phase 2 Must Solve</div>
            <h2 class="p2-section-title">Every problem that must be engineered before this works</h2>
            <p class="p2-section-lead">
                These are not theoretical concerns — they are blocking issues. Each one
                must have a validated engineering solution before the system can be
                considered a working prototype.
            </p>

            <div class="p2-flaw-grid">
                <div class="p2-flaw critical">
                    <div class="p2-flaw-tag">🔴 Critical — Thermodynamic</div>
                    <h4>Net energy deficit: heater draws more than alternator returns</h4>
                    <p>The Rankine cycle efficiency limit (~15–35% for small systems) means you will always burn more energy heating water than you recover from crankshaft output. Battery A must be externally recharged (grid, solar); the alternator loop only extends range, it does not replace the source.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="p2-flaw critical">
                    <div class="p2-flaw-tag">🔴 Critical — Electrical</div>
                    <h4>AC/DC bus instability during load transients</h4>
                    <p>Steam pressure takes seconds to build after heater demand increases. During that lag, the alternator output drops, causing Battery B voltage to sag. A load-shedding priority matrix and minimum SOC thresholds for Bat-B must be enforced in firmware before any cross-charging is allowed.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="p2-flaw critical">
                    <div class="p2-flaw-tag">🔴 Critical — Safety</div>
                    <h4>High-pressure steam containment in a moving vehicle</h4>
                    <p>A 300–600 psi steam failure in a passenger compartment is lethal. ASME Boiler and Pressure Vessel Code (BPVC) Section VIII applies. The reservoir, piping, valves, and fittings must each be rated, inspected, and certified.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="p2-flaw major">
                    <div class="p2-flaw-tag">🟡 Major — Electrical Engineering</div>
                    <h4>Rectifier ripple corrupts PWM control signal</h4>
                    <p>A basic full-wave bridge rectifier produces 100–120 Hz ripple on the DC rail. A properly sized filter capacitor bank plus an LC filter stage is required between the rectifier output and the buck converter input.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="p2-flaw major">
                    <div class="p2-flaw-tag">🟡 Major — Electrical Engineering</div>
                    <h4>Transformer turns ratio and heater impedance mismatch</h4>
                    <p>The transformer must be wound to match the heater's impedance at the operating frequency of the PWM carrier — typically 10–100 kHz — not mains 60 Hz. A custom toroid or E-core is almost certainly required.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="p2-flaw major">
                    <div class="p2-flaw-tag">🟡 Major — Mechanical</div>
                    <h4>Steam piston lubrication failure at operating temperature</h4>
                    <p>Conventional engine oil degrades above ~180°C. Steam engine cylinder oil or solid lubricant coatings on rings must be specified. Oil carry-over into the steam also contaminates the condenser and water reservoir.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="p2-flaw design">
                    <div class="p2-flaw-tag">🟣 Design — Control Systems</div>
                    <h4>No feedback loop between steam pressure and PWM heater duty cycle</h4>
                    <p>A closed-loop PID controller must read reservoir pressure in real time and adjust heater duty cycle to maintain the target pressure setpoint. A pressure transducer, microcontroller, and PID firmware are minimum viable control hardware.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="p2-flaw thermal">
                    <div class="p2-flaw-tag">🟠 Thermal — Physics</div>
                    <h4>Start-up latency: cold system cannot move until steam pressure builds</h4>
                    <p>From a cold start, the heater must bring water from ambient to steam at operating pressure before any torque is available. At 600 W heater input this could take 8–20 minutes. A small 12V starter motor or compressed gas pre-charge may be needed.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="p2-flaw thermal">
                    <div class="p2-flaw-tag">🟠 Thermal — Mass &amp; Packaging</div>
                    <h4>System weight and center-of-gravity shift vs. removed ICE components</h4>
                    <p>Net weight change is likely positive (heavier), and the weight distribution changes significantly. Suspension, braking distances, and handling must be re-evaluated with the actual component weights installed and positioned.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <hr class="p2-sep" />

</div>

